From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 08:24:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(llama-cpp): TTS takes a voice profile's transcript, a
 described voice, or no voice at all

The llama.cpp backend's TTS call knew two kinds of model: one that needs a
speaker reference file (Qwen3-TTS Base: `voice` is a path, required) and one
that takes a named voice through its "tts" chat template (LFM3-Audio). A
model that does both - it clones from a clip AND has a "tts" template to
describe a voice with, or speaks in a voice of its own (OmniVoice) - fit
neither: a request without a voice was refused, a description was read as a
file name, and the transcript the HTTP layer resolves from a voice profile
(params["ref_text"], as the omnivoice-cpp backend reads it) was dropped on
the way.

- tts_request_options: a third mode, reference_or_name. `voice` is the
  reference when it names an existing file (the caller checks; the header
  stays free of I/O), a name or a description for the template otherwise,
  and may be empty. In every mode that describes, the request's
  `instructions` (OpenAI's field) is the template's voice when the request
  names none; with a reference it goes along with it. `ref_text` is kept
  beside a reference - for the model that requires one too.
- grpc-server: the mode comes from the model (mtmd_helper_cap's speaker
  reference flag and whether it has a "tts" template); the transcript goes
  to the helper's speaker_ref_text; the template renders the prompt whenever
  the mode describes a voice.
- prepare.sh: LOCALAI_HAS_TTS_TEMPLATE, a probe for
  common_chat_get_tts_prompt. The "tts" template is older than per-session
  voices (LOCALAI_HAS_TTS_VOICE): at a pin that has the first and not the
  second, a model with a template is now served through it, and
  set_voice stays behind its own probe. A model without a template keeps
  the reference rule there, as before.

- the decoding's seed, steps and guidance: params["seed"], ["flow_steps"]
  and ["cfg_scale"], the three fields upstream's POST /v1/audio/speech
  reads from its body and this call had no way to set. Without a seed the
  helper draws one for every request, so the same text never gave the same
  audio twice; with one it does, and the sampler takes it too. A model
  that decodes in steps under guidance (OmniVoice: 32 steps) can be asked
  for fewer; a model with no steps or guidance refuses a set value itself.

- a voice profile's ENCODED clip kept with the profile (tts_voice_cache.h).
  A model that clones encodes the reference first, about a second on a GPU;
  the engine keeps the encoding in memory, so every load paid it again on
  its first sentence. LocalAI hands a profile's clip to a backend as a
  request-scoped hard link under voice-profiles/.leases/; the backend
  finds the profile's own file by the link's inode and names ONE file
  beside it to the engine (reference.wav.encoded), which reads it when it
  is that clip's by this model and build and writes it otherwise. It sits
  in the profile's directory, with its permissions, and LocalAI's own
  delete (RemoveAll of that directory) removes it: an encoding never
  outlives its voice. A clip that is no lease, a lease that is a copy, a
  profile deleted meanwhile, or a llama.cpp that takes no such file: no
  file is named and the encoding stays in memory, as before.

Nothing changes for Qwen3-TTS Base or LFM3-Audio but the transcript now
reaching the first, and a seed when the request sets one.

Tested: tts_request_options_test.cpp (four new cases and the thirteen
there before) and tts_voice_cache_test.cpp (thirteen cases on a store
built in a temporary directory: the lease, a copy, another layout, a
deleted profile, a link), both standalone and picked up by
backend/cpp/run-unit-tests.sh. The grpc-server change was not compiled
here: it is built with the backend.

